{"benchmark": "diffusion", "args": {"family": "diffusion", "model": "google/diffusiongemma-26B-A4B-it", "device": "auto", "dtype": "bfloat16", "compile": "auto", "expert_parallel": true, "experts_implementation": "by_token_count", "batch_sizes": [1, 4], "variant_batch_size": 1, "variants": [], "prompts": "chat", "prompt_length": 64, "prompt_bucket": 64, "max_new_tokens": 512, "stop_check_interval": 4, "warmup_runs": 2, "timed_runs": 3, "gsm8k_examples": 0, "gsm8k_batch_size": 10, "seed": 0, "label": "diffusion_b1", "output": "/opt/dgemma/repo/results/session2/results.jsonl", "batch_size": 1, "sampler": "static", "cache": "static", "max_denoising_steps": null, "attn_implementation": null, "do_sample": false}, "environment": {"torch": "2.15.0+cpu", "transformers": "5.22.2", "python": "3.12.16", "device": "tpu", "timestamp": "2026-11-10T11:49:22Z", "torch_tpu": "unknown", "device_count": 1}, "load_seconds": 14.137287540999978, "warmup_seconds": [88.24129848199993, 0.549435095999911], "median_tokens_per_second": 859.6337798579972, "median_canvas_tokens_per_second": 859.6337798579972, "median_total_seconds": 0.6324664759999905, "peak_memory_bytes": 18274314752, "runs": [{"num_denoising_steps": 24, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 6, "decoder_forward_passes": [21], "tokens_per_forward": [24.380952835083008], "prefill_seconds": 0.01942988999996942, "canvas_seconds": [0.2661212179999666, 0.24498082899992824], "total_seconds": 0.5328967969999212, "generated_tokens": 512, "canvas_tokens": 512, "tokens_per_second": 997.1431323422136, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 997.1431323422136, "seconds_per_step": 0.021394454458331325, "token_checksum": 26078554, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, you first have to understand why they happen.\n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string or integer) into an index in an underlying array. Because the number of possible keys is often infinite but the size of the array is finite, two different keys will eventually produce the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere are the primary ways hash maps handle these collisions, and how they impact performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Collision Chaining (Separate Addressing)\nThis is the most common method. Instead of storing the value directly in the array slot, each slot (or \"bucket\") contains a **linked list** (or a balanced tree).\n\n*   **How it works:** When a collision occurs at index 5, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at index 5.\n*   **Lookup process:** To find a value, the map hashes the key to find the index, then traverses the list linearly until it finds the matching key.\n*   **Worst-Case Scenario:** If the hash function is poor and every single key hashes to the same index, the hash map effectively turns into one long linked list.\n*   **Worst-Case Time:** **$O(n)$**, where $n$ is the number of elements in the map.\n\n### 2. Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this method, all elements are stored within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map searches for the next available empty slot using a specific strategy.\n\n*   **Linear Probing:** If index $i$ is full, it checks $i+"}, {"num_denoising_steps": 28, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 7, "decoder_forward_passes": [26], "tokens_per_forward": [19.69230842590332], "prefill_seconds": 0.03686400899994169, "canvas_seconds": [0.2659681479999563, 0.32680233799999314], "total_seconds": 0.6324664759999905, "generated_tokens": 512, "canvas_tokens": 512, "tokens_per_second": 859.6337798579972, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 859.6337798579972, "seconds_per_step": 0.02127151667857317, "token_checksum": 25843559, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, you first have to understand the ideal scenario: a hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key into an index in an array. In a perfect world, every key maps to a unique index, making lookups instantaneous (constant time).\n\nHowever, because the set of possible keys is much larger than the size of the array, two different keys will eventually produce the same index. This is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is how hash maps handle these collisions and how it impacts performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Collision Resolution Strategies\n\n#### A. Separate Chaining (The most common method)\nInstead of storing the value directly in the array slot, each slot (or \"bucket\") points to another data structure\u2014usually a **Linked List** or a **Balanced Binary Search Tree**.\n* **How it works:** When a collision occurs at index 5, the new key-value pair is simply appended to the list at index 5.\n* **Lookup:** The map hashes the key, goes to index 5, and then traverses the list linearly until it finds the matching key.\n* **Optimization:** Modern implementations (like Java 8+) convert the linked list into a Red-Black Tree if the list gets too long, keeping search times fast.\n\n#### B. Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map searches for the \"next\" available empty slot.\n* **Linear Probing:** It checks $i+1$, then $i+2$, then $i+3$ until an empty spot is found.\n* *"}, {"num_denoising_steps": 28, "num_canvases": 2, "num_stop_checks": 7, "decoder_forward_passes": [24], "tokens_per_forward": [21.33333396911621], "prefill_seconds": 0.08564832900003694, "canvas_seconds": [0.2666427079999494, 0.3268639780000058], "total_seconds": 0.6820297149999988, "generated_tokens": 512, "canvas_tokens": 512, "tokens_per_second": 858.5110334077945, "canvas_tokens_per_second": 858.5110334077945, "seconds_per_step": 0.02129933521428435, "token_checksum": 26405514, "sample_text": "thought\nTo understand how a hash map handles collisions, you first have to understand why they happen. \n\nA hash map uses a **hash function** to turn a key (like a string) into an integer index in an underlying array. Because the number of possible keys is effectively infinite but the size of the array is finite, two different keys will eventually produce the same index. This event is called a **collision**.\n\nHere is how hash maps manage this problem and how it impacts performance.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Common Collision Resolution Strategies\n\nThere are two primary strategies used to handle collisions:\n\n#### A. Separate Chaining (Open Addressing)\nIn this method, each slot in the array does not store the data directly. Instead, it stores a pointer to another data structure (usually a **Linked List**). \n* **How it works:** If \"Key A\" and \"Key B\" both hash to index 5, index 5 will point to a linked list containing both entries. \n* **Lookup:** To find \"Key B,\" the map goes to index 5 and traverses the list linearly until it finds the entry with the matching key.\n* **Modern Variation:** Many modern implementations (like Java 8+) convert the linked list into a **Balanced Binary Search Tree** if the list grows too large, which keeps performance high.\n\n#### B. Open Addressing (Probing)\nIn this method, all elements are stored directly within the array itself. If a collision occurs, the map looks for the next available empty slot.\n* **Linear Probing:** If index 5 is taken, it checks 6, then 7, th"}]}
